# Trivy ignore list — base-OS CVEs in the RUNTIME image base
# gcr.io/distroless/nodejs22-debian13:nonroot (Debian 13 "trixie"), i.e. what
# `trivy image` actually scans (the node:26-slim build stage is not shipped).
# Every entry: NO fixed version available in trixie — there is literally nothing to
# bump to — and, for all but the one noted below, no runtime path in our Node-only
# proto server (distroless: no shell, no perl, nothing but node + libs).
# Each is an accepted, documented risk; re-review whenever the base image digest
# is bumped (SEC-1 baseline discipline; ties to audit SD-5.1 / F-15).
#
# Reviewed 2026-07-10 (SEC-1): previous entries (bookworm perl/ncurses/zlib-minizip
# from the old node:22-slim single-stage image) no longer apply to the distroless
# runtime and were removed after the trivy-image scan came back to life (the
# corepack build break had silently disabled it — caught by the SEC-1 gate).
#
# Reviewed 2026-08-11 (SEC-22): +2 glibc wordexp entries below. The rest were re-checked
# against the same base image digest and all still apply.
#
# Reviewed 2026-08-17: +1 libssl3t64 QUIC-server entry below. Same base image digest,
# so the earlier entries were not re-verified individually this time.
#
# Reviewed 2026-08-20: +1 libssl3t64 EVP_Cipher/AEAD entry below (CVE-2026-75803). Same
# base image digest again, so the earlier entries were not re-verified individually.
#
# Reviewed 2026-08-24 (SEC-24): +1 libc6 tdelete entry below (CVE-2026-19542). The FIRST
# review at which the base image digest actually MOVED: the `nonroot` tag now resolves to
# sha256:22d2f048…, no longer to the sha256:939d6f16… pinned in the Dockerfile. That rebuild
# is NOT a remediation — both images ship the same libc6 2.41-12+deb13u3, libssl3t64
# 3.5.6-1~deb13u2 and zlib1g 1:1.3.dfsg+really1.3.1-1+b1 (read straight out of
# /var/lib/dpkg/status.d in both amd64 manifests, pulled from gcr.io on 2026-08-24). So the
# "nothing to bump to" verdict of every entry below still holds on substance — but their
# wording "the tag still resolves to the pinned digest" no longer does. Bumping the pin and
# re-verifying the suppressions against the new base is separate work (SEC-25).

# libc6 2.41-12+deb13u3 — MEDIUM, no fixed version published for trixie yet.
CVE-2026-5435
CVE-2026-5450
CVE-2026-5928
CVE-2026-6238

# libc6 — ancient LOW, disputed/won't-fix upstream (Debian marks them minor/unimportant),
# no fixed version; carried by every glibc-based image.
CVE-2010-4756
CVE-2018-20796
CVE-2019-1010022
CVE-2019-1010023
CVE-2019-1010024
CVE-2019-1010025
CVE-2019-9192

# zlib1g 1:1.3.dfsg+really1.3.1-1+b1 — MEDIUM, no fixed version for trixie.
CVE-2026-27171

# libc6 2.41-12+deb13u3 — LOW, both in glibc's wordexp(): CVE-2026-6368 (calling wordexp
# with WRDE_APPEND returns uninitialized memory, a later wordfree may abort the process),
# CVE-2026-6791 (expanding a path that begins with «~username»). Published to the feed
# between 2026-08-10 and 2026-08-11 and, because `trivy image` runs without --severity,
# these two LOW findings alone closed the merge queue for the whole repository.
# Not reachable here: the runtime is distroless (no shell) and the server is Node —
# wordexp()/wordfree() is a C API for shell-style word expansion that neither our code
# nor the Node runtime calls. No fix as of this review: the base image tag `nonroot`
# still resolves to the very sha256:939d6f16… pinned in the Dockerfile, so no rebuilt
# base exists to bump to. Re-review when that digest is bumped.
CVE-2026-6368
CVE-2026-6791

# libssl3t64 3.5.6-1~deb13u2 — MEDIUM, Debian status `fix_deferred` (no fixed version).
# OpenSSL flaw on the QUIC SERVER path ("When an OpenSSL QUIC server (Listener SSL object)
# proce…"). Not reachable here: this image serves HTTP/1.1 + WebSocket over TCP (Fastify,
# `packages/server/src/wsServer.ts` builds an http.Server — or an https.Server when RS-5.1
# native TLS is configured, still TLS-over-TCP), never QUIC/HTTP-3; in the deployed stack
# TLS is normally terminated by Caddy in front. Node exposes no OpenSSL QUIC listener API,
# and the runtime is distroless — no shell and no `openssl` CLI to reach it another way.
# No fix to bump to: the base image tag `nonroot` still resolves to the very
# sha256:939d6f16… pinned in the Dockerfile (checked against gcr.io on 2026-08-17).
# Re-review when that digest is bumped or Debian publishes a fix.
CVE-2026-14456

# libssl3t64 3.5.6-1~deb13u2 — severity UNKNOWN (the feed carries no score yet), and
# "(no fixed version)". OpenSSL, "Check the tag on EVP_Cipher() finalize: Poly1305 and
# OCB AEADs": on the finalize path of the one-shot AEAD API the authentication tag could
# go unchecked.
# Reached the feed AFTER the last green `main` run (2026-08-19 15:50 UTC, security-scan
# run 2716), so it is not something a PR introduced: it turned `trivy image` red on every
# branch at once, and the next weekly run will redden `main` itself. Because the scan runs
# without --severity, this single unscored row closes the merge queue for the whole repo.
# Suppressed NOT because the finding was judged harmless, but because there is nothing to
# fix: no fixed libssl3t64 is published for trixie, and the base image tag `nonroot` still
# resolves to the very sha256:939d6f16… pinned in the Dockerfile (queried against gcr.io
# on 2026-08-20) — no rebuilt base exists to bump to.
# What was verified directly: neither our packages nor the prototype use ChaCha20-Poly1305
# or OCB (no `createCipheriv` and no mention of those ciphers anywhere in the tree), and
# the runtime is distroless — no shell, no `openssl` CLI; in the deployed stack TLS is
# normally terminated by Caddy in front. What could NOT be verified: the advisory text
# itself (security-tracker.debian.org and avd.aquasec.com are unreachable from the dev
# environment), so a path through OpenSSL's own ChaCha20-Poly1305 TLS ciphersuite cannot
# be ruled out. The risk is therefore accepted for a SHORT window, not open-endedly.
# Re-review 2026-09-03 — or sooner, as soon as Debian publishes a fix or the base image
# digest is bumped. If a fix appears, this entry is REMOVED, not extended.
CVE-2026-75803

# libc6 2.41-12+deb13u3 — severity UNKNOWN (Debian has not scored it) and "(no fixed
# version)". glibc, "Out-of-bounds stack array access in tdelete" — the POSIX binary-search-
# tree API (<search.h>: tsearch/tfind/tdelete/twalk).
# `main` went red WITHOUT a code change, and this time that is provable without a re-run:
# the very same commit 671c03f6 was scanned GREEN by the push run 32628248009 (2026-08-23
# 08:26 UTC) and RED by the weekly scheduled run 32695151718 (2026-08-24 05:53 UTC). Only
# the CVE feed moved between them — which is exactly what the weekly re-scan of `main`
# exists to catch. Because `trivy image` runs without --severity, this single unscored row
# closed the merge queue for the whole repository again (#777, a docs-only PR, was the one
# left blocked).
# Suppressed NOT because the finding was judged harmless, but because there is nothing to
# fix: no fixed libc6 is published for trixie (Trivy's Fixed Version column is empty), and
# the rebuilt base image does not carry one either — see the 2026-08-24 review note above.
# What was verified directly: nothing in this repo uses the tsearch/tdelete family (it is a
# C API, and there is no reference to it anywhere in packages/ or prototype/), and the
# runtime is distroless — no shell, no C tooling to reach it another way. What could NOT be
# verified: the advisory text itself (security-tracker.debian.org, api.osv.dev and
# avd.aquasec.com are all blocked by the dev environment's egress proxy), so a glibc-
# INTERNAL caller of tdelete cannot be ruled out from here. The risk is therefore accepted
# for a SHORT window, not open-endedly.
# Re-review 2026-09-07 — or sooner, as soon as Debian publishes a fix. If a fix appears,
# this entry is REMOVED, not extended.
CVE-2026-19542
